  "summary": "Traditional Lithuanian wooden crosses featuring intricate carvings and symbolic ornamentation.",
  "description": "Characterized by elaborate geometric patterns, floral motifs, and religious symbols carved into wood, often with small metal decorations and painted elements. These crosses serve as both religious objects and expressions of cultural identity, with distinctive regional styles and techniques.",
  "culturalContext": "Cross-crafting fused Catholic votive need with Lithuania's pre-Christian sacred-forest memory; the Hill of Crosses survived Soviet bulldozers to become the emblem of peaceful resistance (John Paul II visited 1993), and UNESCO's 2001 listing protects the traveling masters who still raise sun-crowned crosses at roadsides and graves.",
